    "result_text": "Done. The full build and test suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1 tests green), and the audit log confirms the new identity in action: `customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`.\n\n## What changed\n\n**New customerCode = `<REGION>-<HASH8>`** (sequence numbers dropped). Region comes from the postcode; HASH8 is the first 8 upper-case hex chars of `SHA-256(normalizedTelephone + lastName)`.\n\nI split the work into small single-responsibility units and reused existing pieces rather than duplicating:\n\n- **`util/Sha256.java`** (new) \u2014 one SHA-256 hex helper. `Household` now delegates to it instead of carrying its own private copy of the digest code (removed duplication).\n- **`util/OwnerRegion.java`** (new) \u2014 the single source of an owner's region (postcode range first, city-table fallback, `UNKNOWN`). This is exactly the derivation `OwnerMapper.locality` used to inline.\n- **`util/CustomerCode.java`** (new) \u2014 builds `<REGION>-<HASH8>` and extracts the region segment back out.\n- **`AssignOwnerCustomerCode`** \u2014 now purely deterministic from the owner's region + identity; no longer reads the repository/per-city count.\n- **`OwnerMapper.locality`** \u2014 now reads the REGION segment off the `customerCode` (the \"region-and-hash identity\"), falling back to `OwnerRegion` only when no code is present. Because the region is derived the same way as before, the locality *value* is unchanged for pipeline-created owners.\n\nEverything built *from* the customerCode follows automatically:\n- **membershipNumber** (`<customerCode>-M<YY>`) \u2192 now e.g. `NSW-1A2B3C4D-M26`.\n- **checkDigit** (Luhn over the code's digits) \u2192 recomputes over the hex hash.\n- **create audit record** (`AuditOwnerCreated`) \u2192 logs the new code.\n\nI also updated the doc/comments that pinned the old format: the `owners.POST.yml` step comment, the `openapi.yml` descriptions/examples for `customerCode`, `membershipNumber`, and `locality`, and the `Luhn` javadoc example.\n\nOne design note worth flagging: the spec says \"REGION is the region code derived from the postcode.\" I made REGION use the app's existing region derivation (postcode-first, city fallback, `UNKNOWN`) \u2014 the same logic that previously produced `locality` \u2014 so that locality can genuinely *move onto* the identity without changing any owner's resolved region value. If you'd rather REGION be strictly postcode-only (yielding `UNKNOWN` when a city-only owner has no postcode, changing their locality), that's a one-line change in `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`.",
